  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Layla and her spellings",
      "content": "<p>Layla is Arabic for night, and it reaches England and Wales in several spellings: Layla (the most common), Leila (the usual Persian form, made familiar in English by Byron), Laila and Leyla (Turkish). They're all the same name. Lyla, Lilah and Lila are harder: each can be a form of Leila, but each also has another source, so we've marked them uncertain.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Dark names from Ireland and Scotland",
      "content": "<p>Irish <em>ciar</em> ('black') and <em>dub</em> ('dark') are behind a whole set of names. From <em>ciar</em> come Ciar, Ciara, Kiera and Keira for girls and Ciarán and Kieran for boys; from <em>dub</em> come Dougal ('dark stranger'), Duff and Dubhán.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Evening names",
      "content": "<p>If 'night' feels too dark, evening names are gentler. Vesper and Hesperos are the Latin and Greek names for the evening star, Ilta is Finnish for evening, and Samar and Samir are Arabic names about talking together in the evening. Odhiambo, from Luo in Kenya, is given to a child born in the evening.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What name means night?",
      "answer": "Layla (and Leila, Laila, Leyla) means night in Arabic. Nyx is Greek and Nox Latin for night, Nisha and Yamini mean night in Sanskrit, and Tuta means night in Quechua."
    },
    {
      "question": "What names mean night or darkness?",
      "answer": "For darkness: Melanie ('black, dark'), Kieran and Ciarán ('little dark one'), Ciara and Keira, Dougal ('dark stranger'), Krishna ('black, dark') and Erebus ('nether darkness'). For night: Layla, Nyx, Nox and Nisha."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means night in Arabic?",
      "answer": "Layla, which is also written Leila, Laila and Leyla. Asra means 'travel at night', and Samar 'evening conversation'."
    }
  ],
